There was a greater sense of consensus, on-stage and off-, about most of the rest of the 22 songs played in Oakland. The first chosen was &#8220;Raising Hell,&#8221; a rowdy oldie that made it clear that, among other things, Carlile and the twins have always harbored stomp-and-holler tendencies&#8230; with literal stomping, on a few occasions in this set, to add percussion into the mix. In the most extreme pendulum swing possible, next up among the requests was a song about raising heaven, &#8220;The Mother,&#8221; a gorgeously happy song that deals with motherhood very generally and gay motherhood very specifically.

Carlile preceded &#8220;The Mother&#8221; with an extended anecdote about chaperoning the social life of her eldest daughter, who's repeatedly named in the chorus &#x2014; with a side note that the story should not leave the room, since Evangeline is at the age where it's not easy to always discern whether she resents being talked about on stage or takes pride in it in any given moment. That alone, in the singer's mind, justified the decision to have phones put away in Yondr pouches, something Carlile has not required at any of her previous gigs but wanted for these, both to create greater intimacy and to keep flubs from going up on YouTube. (There weren't any blatant ones, although the Hanseroths did shoot knowing smiles at each other a couple of times when a note sounded a little bit interesting.)

Carlile has often spoken before &#8220;The Mother&#8221; in concert, but the loose formatting of this show seemed to allow her to stretch out even more, over a period of minutes. &#8220;I remember having a really difficult time with it because Catherine carried both girls&#8230; Somebody talked me through it and told me I needed to come up with a mantra. And my mantra became, I have to tell myself I'm a mother so that I can believe I deserve to call myself that. So it became &#8216;I am the mother of Evangeline.' And when I first wrote this song, I thought, &#8216;Well, this thing's niche! You have to be a gay mom that didn't carry a child to understand this song.' And it's not true &#x2014; it's a universal thing &#x2014; because here I am in Oakland, and this man stood up and wanted me to play the song.&#8221;

