Dolly Parton was saluted with &#8220;Jolene&#8221; &#x2014; &#8220;She had a temper, she had a dirty mouth, and she had the biggest heart in the world,&#8221; said Carlile &#x2014; and a call-out for some Joni Mitchell on that singer's birthday went rewarded with an E-major &#8220;River.&#8221; The singer talked about a two-and-a-half-hour phone call while driving home on Halloween night, hearing even more wild stories from the legend than she'd already heard. &#8220;A lot of times she'll end the story by going, &#8216;And that was where I found &#8220;Refuge of the Roads.&#8221;&#8216; Or, &#8216;And that's why I shouldn't have got on that plane.' In those moments, I realized that, no matter what happens in my life, I got to listen to Joni Mitchell play solitaire and tell me her stories. She's not lying. I'm not even sure she's capable of it.&#8221;

The last stretch of the Oakland concert lifted the show from delightful travelogue to a something heavier on some of the trio's most emotionally incisive songs, although it was difficult to know whether they were making these selections intentionally or those requests were providentially just arriving right on time. Tim Hanseroth did say he'd been waiting all night to pick on the guy in the front rows holding a sign asking for &#8220;The Things I Regret,&#8221; and subsequently showed up on Instagram thanking the fan again. It raised one of those questions that can only arise at a rarities-filled show like this: Why was this not a bigger hit &#x2014; even amongst the band itself? It's got an unforgettable singalong hook and serious stomp-and-holler bona fides, and it's about the accumulation of screw-ups that adds up to a lifetime's worth. What more could you ask?

